A project transfer moves a NAHPU project—with its records and media—from one
installation to another. `Export project` writes the archive; the receiving
device reads it in one of two ways:

| On the receiving device | Use | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The project does not exist yet | `Import project` on the home screen | A new local project |
| The project already exists | `Merge project` in that project | Imported records combined with existing ones |

Both flows accept a project export or a NAHPU Data Package, which carries the
same project transfer payload. Record tables, Darwin Core bundles, and
documents cannot restore a project. Importing a project-info JSON or QR code
creates only the project's name and metadata, without records or media. To
replace the entire installation instead, see
[Restoring a database](/en/usages/export/#restoring-a-database).

## Export a project {% #export-a-project %}

{% steps %}

1. Open the project on the source device.
2. Open the `Dashboard` menu and select `Export project`.
3. Review `Archive contents` {% ref id="C" /%}: counts for personnel, taxonomy,
   sites, events, specimens, narratives, and media, plus total `Media size`
   (when media are included) and any missing-media warnings.
4. Choose what to include:
   - For a full transfer or backup, leave `Light export` off and choose
     `TAR.GZ` (default) or `ZIP` {% ref id="A" /%}. Media are included.
   - Over a slow connection, turn on `Light export` {% ref id="B" /%}. NAHPU
     writes a smaller `JSON.GZ` file without media.
5. Check the `File name` (optionally turn on `Append date`) and choose a
   destination folder on desktop.
6. Select `Export project` {% ref id="D" /%}. You can cancel while the archive
   is being built. Once finished, share, open the folder, or save a copy.

{% /steps %}

### What a project export contains

- Every record belonging to the project, including specimen parts, symbionts,
  custom fields, and each specimen's taxon-specific attributes (for
  invertebrates: ecological interactions, environmental parameters, optional
  morphometrics, sex, and remarks).
- Only the taxa referenced by the project's specimens.
- Personnel photos, project media, and taxon media present on disk. Any missing
  file is listed as a warning and its file link is left out.

## Import onto another installation {% #import-a-project %}

Use `Import project` to set up a project on a new device or recover it on a
replacement device.

{% steps %}

1. Keep the source archive and back up any existing data on the receiving
   device.
2. On the home screen menu, select `Import project`, then `Choose archive`.
   Pick a project `JSON.GZ`, `ZIP`, or `TAR.GZ` file, or a NAHPU Data Package.
3. Check the project details and `Destination project name`. If the project
   already exists on this device, select `Open Merge project` instead. A
   different project sharing the same name needs a unique destination name.
4. Acknowledge the backup reminder. Review personnel, taxonomy, and specimens,
   and resolve every item marked `Needs attention`.
5. Review the final summary and any missing-file warnings, then select
   `Import project`.
6. Open the imported project and spot-check sites, events, specimen IDs,
   narratives, and attachments.

{% /steps %}

A `JSON.GZ` file contains no media. Keep the source archive until you have
verified the imported project.

## Merge into the matching project {% #merge-into-the-matching-project %}

Use `Merge project` to combine a teammate's copy of the project with yours.

{% aside type="caution" %}

Back up the database before merging. The wizard offers `Back up now` {% ref id="F" /%}
and requires you to acknowledge the backup reminder {% ref id="G" /%}.

{% /aside %}

{% steps %}

1. Open your copy of the project.
2. Open the `Dashboard` menu and select `Merge project`.
3. Select `Choose archive` {% ref id="E" /%} and pick a `JSON.GZ`, `ZIP`, or
   `TAR.GZ` project export, or a NAHPU Data Package.
4. Acknowledge the backup reminder {% ref id="G" /%}.
5. Review each section in turn: project info, personnel, taxonomy, sites,
   events, specimens, and narratives.
6. Choose how to handle each match or conflict.
7. Check the final summary and select `Merge project`.

{% /steps %}

Nothing is written while you step through the wizard. NAHPU commits all changes
together at the final step; if the merge fails, the database stays unchanged
and newly copied media files are cleaned up.

## Resolve conflicts {% #resolve-conflicts %}

When an imported record matches one already on this device, NAHPU keeps yours
unless you choose otherwise:

| Choice | What happens |
| --- | --- |
| **Keep current** | Your record stays. Imported records that depend on it link to yours. |
| **Use imported** | The imported record replaces yours, along with the data it owns. |
| **Import as new** | The imported record is added separately, with a new UUID if needed and a unique suffix on duplicate site IDs. |
| **Skip** | The record is not imported. Dependent records that cannot be relinked are skipped too. |

### How records are matched

| Record | Matched by |
| --- | --- |
| Project info | Compared field by field |
| Personnel, specimens | UUID |
| Taxonomy | Genus and specific epithet |
| Sites | Site ID (when not empty) |
| Events | Site, start date, and ID suffix (start time is ignored) |
| Narratives | Site, date, time, and writer |

Personnel and taxa are shared across every project on the device, so the wizard
warns whenever replacing one could affect another project. A specimen UUID
belonging to a different project cannot be replaced; import it as new or skip
it.

## Force merge a different project UUID {% #force-merge-a-different-project-uuid %}

Merging normally requires the archive's project UUID to match the open project.
A mismatch is blocked because it usually means the wrong project is open.

If both archives genuinely belong to the same field project, turn on
`Force merge into the active project` and type the open project's name to
confirm. NAHPU reassigns the imported records to the open project's UUID.
Conflicts are never overwritten automatically; every conflict choice above
still applies.
